(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2809070100-clojure-java-io-capability-boundary-proposal")
  (:adr/title "Accepted design for the clojure.java.io -> kotoba-lang/fs + kotoba-lang/io migration: concrete before/after for 8 sampled call shapes, gap list, rollout sequencing, and an explicit non-migrating-cleanly section")
   (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-07")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2809061500-clojure-namespace-to-kotoba-stdlib" "adr-2608260200-kotoba-lang-external-dependency-substitution"))
  (:adr/context
  "adr-2809061500 named clojure.java.io as the EXPAND-WITH-REDESIGN namespace, scheduled last of the eight, because real clojure.java.io is a set of ambient-OS free functions (io/reader, io/writer, io/file, io/resource, io/copy, io/input-stream, io/output-stream -- any of which can be called from anywhere with any path or URL) while kotoba-lang/fs + kotoba-lang/io are deliberately capability-injected: code cannot reach for the filesystem ambiently, a host must hand it an IFilesystem/IReader/IWriter handle first. That ADR flagged this as the single most invasive of the eight namespaces (3,320 require sites, workspace-wide) and explicitly deferred the redesign to a follow-up. This ADR is that follow-up. It is a DESIGN PROPOSAL only -- no kotoba-lang/fs or kotoba-lang/io source was touched to produce it, per explicit task scope, because doing the actual 3,320-site migration wrong at this scale would be a serious mistake and needs owner review first. :adr/status is proposed, not accepted.\n\nWhat kotoba-lang/fs actually provides today (read at /tmp/fs, commit at time of writing): kotoba.lang.fs is pure -- path ops (join/split/basename/dirname/normalize/ext/absolute?/relative?) plus the IFilesystem protocol (read/read-bytes/write/list/exists?/delete) and IAsyncFilesystem (the same five, -async suffixed, returning CompletableFuture on JVM / Promise on cljs), plus mem-filesystem, an atom-backed in-memory impl for tests. kotoba.lang.fs-host (a separate namespace, deliberately: fs.cljc must stay free of java.nio/node:fs so a kotoba-WASM guest can require it) supplies host-filesystem and async-host-filesystem: a real OS-backed IFilesystem confined to a REQUIRED :root (no default -- not CWD, not $HOME, not /tmp), with typed refusals (:fs/escape, :fs/absolute, :fs/home-escape, :fs/path-too-long, :fs/null-byte, :fs/backslash, :fs/not-found, :fs/too-large, :fs/is-directory, :fs/not-a-directory, :fs/io, :fs/bad-root) instead of a caught java.io.IOException, and a :max-bytes bound (default 1 MiB) checked from stat before any bytes are loaded. read/write operate on whole-file string content, not streams. list is single-level (returns child names of a directory), not a recursive walk. There is no rename/move primitive, no symlink-check primitive exposed to callers (fs-host does the check internally and refuses), and no POSIX-permission API.\n\nWhat kotoba-lang/io actually provides today (read at /tmp/io): the IReader (read! -> next byte-array chunk or nil at EOF) and IWriter (write! -> append a chunk) protocols, a pure byte-buffer (persistent-vector-backed, portable to WASM), reader-buffer/buffer-writer to adapt a byte-buffer as an IReader/IWriter, copy (drain a reader into a writer, a pure reduction with no threads -- the host or caller drives the loop), and reader-seq (a lazy seq of chunks). Critically: kotoba.lang.fs's IFilesystem does NOT implement IReader/IWriter -- it is whole-content read/write, not a source of chunks -- so `io/copy` from real clojure.java.io (which streams between any Reader/Writer/InputStream/OutputStream/File/URL/byte[]/String pair without loading the whole thing into memory) has no direct kotoba-lang equivalent yet for the file-to-file or stream-to-file shapes; only the fully-in-memory buffer-to-buffer shape is covered. There is also no classpath-resource-loading capability anywhere in kotoba-lang/fs or kotoba-lang/io: io/resource is a JVM classloader concept (find a file bundled inside a JAR or on the classpath) with no notion of a 'root' to inject, and no obvious portable equivalent exists for a kotoba-WASM guest, which has no classpath at all.\n\nSample selection (Step 2 of the task): grepped `orgs/` (3,517 files containing clojure.java.io) for a diverse set of real, currently-checked-out call sites -- not synthetic examples -- covering: simple resource load (2x), self-locating classpath discovery, atomic file-to-file write via a copy, in-memory content-hashing via input-stream-to-ByteArrayOutputStream copy, classpath-resource-to-tempfile materialization for a subprocess, subprocess stdin/stdout/stderr piping via reader/writer over process streams (a non-file source), and a private-signing-key file with POSIX permission bits. All 8 are read in full below with their actual surrounding code, not just the require line.")
  (:adr/decision
  "(a) BEFORE/AFTER for the 8 sampled patterns.\n\n1. RESOURCE LOAD (test fixture). Site: orgs/gftdcojp/ai-gftd-cybersecurity/test/ai_gftd/cybersecurity/agent_test.clj:9.\n   BEFORE: `(-> \"operations/drivers.edn\" io/resource slurp edn/read-string)`\n   AFTER (host-injected): the test namespace stops calling io/resource itself. A test-support helper in the repo's own test harness constructs `(fs-host/host-filesystem {:root (str (io/file (System/getProperty \"user.dir\") \"test-resources\"))})` ONCE at suite setup (this one seam is allowed to touch java.io directly, the same way `kotoba.lang.fs-host` itself is allowed to, because a test runner IS the host here), and the test body becomes `(edn/read-string (fs/read fixture-fs \"operations/drivers.edn\"))`. This requires the fixture to be copied from `test/resources/operations/drivers.edn` (classpath) to a `test-resources/` directory that fs-host's :root can point at, OR (preferred, no file duplication) a new `kotoba.lang.fs-classpath` adapter, described in (b) below, that hands back a read-only IFilesystem rooted at a classpath directory.\n\n2. SELF-LOCATING CLASSPATH DISCOVERY. Site: orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba-actors/src/kotoba_actors/config.cljc:38-44.\n   BEFORE:\n   ```\n   (some-> (io/resource \"kotoba_actors/config.cljc\") io/file .getParentFile .getParentFile .getParentFile .getPath)\n   ```\n   used to find *this repository's own checkout root* by walking up from its compiled location on the classpath, in order to derive a sibling repository's path (`../../etzhayyim`).\n   AFTER: this is the one pattern in the sample that does NOT get a capability-injected replacement -- see (d). It is left on clojure.java.io with a documented exception, because it is not asking a granted handle to read named data; it is asking 'where does the classloader think this jar/dir is', which is the definition of ambient authority this whole redesign exists to remove. The honest fix is architectural, not a wrapper: `kotoba-actors.config` should stop deriving the sibling root by classpath introspection and instead require it as an explicit host-supplied :root (an env var it already partially supports, KOTOBA_ACTORS_ETZHAYYIM_ROOT) with NO classpath-walk fallback -- the file's own docstring already documents that the classpath-walk fallback produces a 'plausible-looking WRONG path' off the expected layout, which is exactly the failure mode capability injection is designed to make impossible by construction rather than by comment.\n\n3. ATOMIC FILE-TO-FILE WRITE VIA COPY. Site: orgs/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-6202/src/svcdesk/render_html.clj:119-131.\n   BEFORE:\n   ```\n   (clojure.java.io/make-parents out)\n   (spit tmp html)\n   (if (= (slurp out) html) (skip) (clojure.java.io/copy (clojure.java.io/file tmp) (clojure.java.io/file out)))\n   (clojure.java.io/delete-file tmp :silently)\n   ```\n   AFTER: `-main` takes a granted `fs` handle (an fs-host/host-filesystem rooted at the repo's docs/samples dir, constructed by the host/CLI entrypoint that calls -main, not by -main itself):\n   ```\n   (fs/write fs tmp-name html)\n   (let [prev (try (fs/read fs out-name) (catch #?(:clj Exception :cljs :default) _ nil))]\n     (when (not= prev html)\n       (fs/write fs out-name html)\n       (println (str \"wrote: \" out-name))))\n   (fs/delete fs tmp-name)\n   ```\n   Note this drops the tmp-then-rename dance entirely, because fs/write is a single whole-content write, not two syscalls -- but that also means it is NOT crash-atomic the way `Files/move ATOMIC_MOVE` is (see gap list in (b) and the folder_sync.clj discussion in (c)/(d)). For a build-report script (this site) that is an acceptable trade; for the load-bearing case in pattern 8 it is not, which is exactly why rollout order in (c) puts this kind of script first and that kind of durable-state write last.\n\n4. INPUT-STREAM-TO-BYTEARRAYOUTPUTSTREAM (content hashing). Site: orgs/kotoba-lang/chie/src/etzhayyim/kotoba/cid.cljc:133-141 (already a .cljc file with a `:cljs (throw (ex-info \"cljs not wired\" {}))` stub -- exactly the kind of file this migration should fix, not just port).\n   BEFORE:\n   ```\n   (defn cid-of-file [path]\n     #?(:clj (with-open [in (clojure.java.io/input-stream path) out (java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream.)]\n               (clojure.java.io/copy in out)\n               (cid (.toByteArray out)))\n        :cljs (throw (ex-info \"cljs not wired\" {}))))\n   ```\n   AFTER (portable on both runtimes, no #?() branch needed in this function at all): `cid-of-file` takes an injected `fs` handle instead of a bare path:\n   ```\n   (defn cid-of-file [fs path] (cid (fs/read-bytes fs path)))\n   ```\n   `fs/read-bytes` already exists on the mem-filesystem impl in kotoba-lang/fs (returns a vector of unsigned ints); it is NOT yet implemented in fs-host's host-filesystem (see gap list). Once added there (a small addition -- host-filesystem already reads UTF-8 string content; read-bytes needs the raw byte path, which JVM already computes internally via Files/readAllBytes before the String conversion), this call site becomes fully portable and the cljs stub disappears -- a real win from doing the redesign properly rather than a like-for-like port.\n\n5. CLASSPATH-RESOURCE-TO-TEMPFILE FOR A SUBPROCESS. Site: orgs/kotoba-lang/provider/src/provider/edn_codec.clj:44-54 (load-bearing: materializes a wasm package from the classpath so a Node subprocess, which needs a real path, can load it).\n   BEFORE:\n   ```\n   (defn- resource-file [resource]\n     (let [url (io/resource resource)]\n       (when-not url (throw ...))\n       (let [tmp (File/createTempFile \"kotoba-edn-codec-\" \".wasm\")]\n         (.deleteOnExit tmp)\n         (io/copy (io/input-stream url) tmp)\n         (.getAbsolutePath tmp))))\n   ```\n   AFTER: this site needs BOTH a classpath-resource capability (gap, see (b) and (d)) AND a temp-directory-scoped fs handle. Given both: `provider.edn-codec` accepts an injected `{:keys [resource-fs tmp-fs]}` at construction (from its own host, e.g. the compiler CLI), and the function becomes `(let [bytes (fs/read-bytes resource-fs resource)] (fs/write tmp-fs tmp-name bytes) (fs/path tmp-fs tmp-name))` -- i.e. it stops calling File/createTempFile itself (ambient temp-dir authority) and instead the host grants a tmp-fs rooted at a directory the host controls and is responsible for cleaning up (replacing .deleteOnExit, which also has no capability-injected equivalent -- see (d)).\n\n6. SUBPROCESS STDIN/STDOUT/STDERR PIPING (non-file reader/writer). Site: orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba-code/src/kotoba_code/host.clj:151-185, 223.\n   BEFORE:\n   ```\n   (def pump-out (future (io/copy (.getInputStream p) out)))\n   (def pump-err (future (io/copy (.getErrorStream p) err)))\n   (when in (with-open [w (io/writer (.getOutputStream p))] (.write w (str in))))\n   ...\n   (with-open [r (io/reader f)] (loop [...] (.readLine r) ...))\n   ```\n   AFTER: this is the strongest existing match to kotoba-lang/io's actual shape, because io/copy there is ALREADY `(reader writer) -> nil, draining read! into write!` with no threads, matching this site's spirit (host-driven loop) more closely than clojure.java.io's thread-based io/copy does. The process's stdout/stderr InputStreams get wrapped by a small host-owned adapter (NOT yet in kotoba-lang/io -- see gap list) implementing IReader over `java.io.InputStream`/Node readable stream chunk-by-chunk, and the subprocess's stdin OutputStream gets a matching IWriter adapter; `kotoba.lang.io/copy` then drains them exactly as it does today for byte-buffers. This is the one sampled site that is entirely process-management, not filesystem, and it argues that kotoba-lang/io's IReader/IWriter -- not kotoba-lang/fs -- is the natural home for process-stream adapters (they belong beside `kotoba-lang/process`'s existing validate-spawn policy layer, referenced in adr-2809061500, not inside fs).\n\n7. LINE-NUMBERED BOUNDED FILE READ. Site: orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba-code/src/kotoba_code/host.clj:211-223 (already has its own `max-read-lines`/`max-read-bytes` limits -- this file is halfway to the capability-injected shape already, which is a useful signal that the target design is a natural fit for exactly this kind of tool-sandbox code).\n   BEFORE: `(with-open [r (io/reader f)] (loop [...] (.readLine r) ...))` truncating at `max-read-lines`.\n   AFTER: `(let [content (fs/read fs path)] (->> (str/split-lines content) (take max-read-lines) ...))` -- because fs-host already bounds the whole read at :max-bytes (checked from stat before any bytes load), the two limits (max-read-bytes at the fs layer, max-read-lines at the caller layer) compose cleanly instead of requiring the caller to do its own line-by-line streaming to avoid loading an oversized file; fs-host's existing bound already prevents that.\n\n8. PRIVATE KEY FILE, READ/WRITE WITH POSIX PERMISSIONS. Site: orgs/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-app/src/cloud/itonami/app/capability.clj:57-70 (production: this Drive-issuer Ed25519 seed backs every capability the app mints; the file lives beside the app's live state directory).\n   BEFORE:\n   ```\n   (defn- key-file [] (io/file (config/data-dir) \"drive-issuer.key\"))\n   (defn- read-seed [] (let [f (key-file)] (when (.isFile f) (.decode (Base64/getDecoder) (str/trim (slurp f))))))\n   (defn- write-seed! [seed] (let [f (key-file)] (.mkdirs (.getParentFile f)) (spit f (.encodeToString (Base64/getEncoder) seed)) (doto f (.setReadable false false) (.setReadable true true) (.setWritable false false) (.setWritable true true)) seed))\n   ```\n   AFTER (read/write half only -- see (d) for the permission half): the app's host wiring constructs `(fs-host/host-filesystem {:root (config/data-dir)})` once, at startup, and hands it to `cloud.itonami.app.capability` at construction (this module already receives `config` by reference, so this is a small, mechanical change to its call sites, not a rewrite of its logic): `(fs/read fs \"drive-issuer.key\")` / `(fs/write fs \"drive-issuer.key\" encoded)`, decoding Base64 on either side of the same boundary as today. The `.isFile` existence check becomes an `(fs/exists? fs \"drive-issuer.key\")` guard, or a try/catch on the typed `:fs/not-found` refusal from fs/read -- either is a direct, faithful port.\n\n(b) NEW PRIMITIVES NEEDED vs. ALREADY COVERED.\n\nAlready covered by the existing surface, no changes needed: whole-file read/write/list/exists?/delete (patterns 1 partial, 3, 8) via fs + fs-host as they exist today; in-memory buffer copy (the byte-buffer half of pattern 4's spirit) via kotoba-lang/io as it exists today.\n\nGaps that need new primitives, ranked by how many sampled sites they unblock:\n  - `fs/read-bytes` on fs-host's host-filesystem (JVM and Node): the protocol already declares it and mem-filesystem already implements it; fs-host's host-filesystem does not. Unblocks pattern 4 outright and is a small, low-risk addition (the JVM branch already computes a raw byte[] internally before the UTF-8 String conversion in `read`; Node's readFileSync already supports a no-encoding call). This should be the first thing added, before any rewire wave touches java.io/input-stream call sites.\n  - A classpath-resource-backed IFilesystem, e.g. `kotoba.lang.fs-classpath/classpath-filesystem` (a third sibling namespace beside fs-host, same separation principle: fs.cljc stays pure, fs-host wraps java.nio/node:fs, fs-classpath wraps the JVM ClassLoader / a bundled resource map). JVM-only realistically (no classpath concept in cljs/kotoba-WASM); cljs/WASM callers would receive this capability pre-populated as a mem-filesystem built at build time instead (the host bakes the resource bytes in, rather than the guest discovering them). Unblocks patterns 1, 2 (partially -- see (d)), 5.\n  - A stream-chunk IReader/IWriter adapter over `java.io.InputStream`/`OutputStream` (JVM) and Node Readable/Writable streams, living in kotoba-lang/io beside byte-buffer (NOT in kotoba-lang/fs, since it is not filesystem-specific -- process pipes and network sockets need the identical adapter). Unblocks pattern 6, and is also what `kotoba-lang/process`'s eventual spawn transport (named as a TODO in adr-2809061500) will need regardless of this ADR.\n  - A bounded IFilesystem-to-IFilesystem or IFilesystem-to-tempfile `copy` helper that streams rather than materializing the whole file as a Clojure string in memory, for the file-to-file case (pattern 3, 5) once files exceed a size where whole-string materialization is wasteful. Not urgent (the existing :max-bytes bound already keeps worst-case memory use bounded and known), but worth naming as a follow-up rather than silently declaring the whole-content approach permanent.\n  - Recursive directory walk (`fs/list` is single-level only) and an atomic rename/move primitive: not needed by any of the 8 sampled sites directly, but needed by the very next site examined beyond the sample (`cloud-itonami-app/folder_sync.clj`, read in the course of this investigation), which hand-rolls both a `Files/walk` tree traversal and a `Files/move ATOMIC_MOVE` temp-file-then-rename, WITH its own symlink-escape check that duplicates exactly what fs-host already centralizes for single-path resolution. This is flagged here because it is the strongest evidence in the whole sample that centralizing fs-host's root-confinement logic has real value beyond this migration -- folder_sync.clj is currently reimplementing (correctly, but independently) the same escape-refusal fs-host already proves once.\n\n(c) ROLLOUT SEQUENCING.\n\nLowest risk first -- build/one-shot/dev-tooling scripts whose worst failure mode is a bad build artifact, not corrupted or leaked production data, and which are already single-purpose enough that constructing one fs-host handle at their entry point costs little: pattern 3 (render_html.clj, a docs-sample generator invoked by a human or CI, not by any live request path), pattern 1 (test fixtures -- a test that fails loudly on a bad path is the cheapest possible place to discover a migration mistake), and pattern 4 (cid.cljc -- pure content-hashing utility, easy to parity-test against the old java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream output byte-for-byte before cutover, and the fix is a genuine improvement, not just a port). These three, plus the general class they represent (one-shot scripts under scripts/, test/, and docs generators), should form the first migration wave.\n\nMiddle risk: pattern 7 (kotoba-code/host.clj's bounded file read) and pattern 5 (provider/edn_codec.clj's resource materialization) -- both are inside a tool/compiler-bridge path that a human invokes interactively or a CI job invokes non-interactively, not inside a live network request path, but both feed real build/agent-tool outputs that other code trusts, so they need the read-bytes and classpath-resource primitives (b) landed and parity-tested FIRST, then a migration with before/after golden-output comparison (same bytes in, same bytes/behavior out) rather than a rewrite-and-hope.\n\nHighest risk, migrate last: pattern 6 (kotoba-code/host.clj's subprocess piping -- concurrency-sensitive, thread/future-based today, and a botched port could deadlock or silently truncate agent tool output) and pattern 8 (cloud-itonami-app/capability.clj's signing-key file -- this is the one sampled site that is genuinely load-bearing inside a production app's request path: every capability the cloud-itonami Drive issues is signed with this key, so a migration bug here does not just fail loudly, it could (i) regenerate a fresh key when the old one should have been read, silently invalidating every capability already issued -- exactly the failure mode the file's own docstring already warns against for the CURRENT code, meaning any replacement must preserve that same 'never silently regenerate' guarantee under the new error-handling shape (:fs/not-found is not :fs/bad-root; the port must map the typed refusal it actually wants to treat as 'no key yet' correctly), or (ii) fail to reproduce the file's own POSIX-permission tightening (see (d)), silently leaving key material world-readable after migration where it was not before. Because this ADR is specifically about clojure.java.io and cloud-itonami-app is itself a live actor with its own governor/CACAO pattern (see this workspace's build-actor skill and the actor's own repository), any change here should follow that repo's own review process in addition to this program's, and land only after the read-bytes/classpath-resource primitives have already been proven on the lower-risk waves above. The general principle this ordering follows: order by (i) whether the code path is invoked by a live request or only by a human/CI trigger, and (ii) whether a wrong answer is caught immediately (a test fails, a build artifact looks visibly wrong) or silently (a key silently regenerates, permissions silently loosen, an agent tool silently truncates output) -- live + silent-failure sites go last, non-live + loud-failure sites go first.\n\n(d) WHAT DOES NOT MIGRATE CLEANLY.\n\n  - Pattern 2 (self-locating classpath discovery, kotoba-actors/config.cljc). No capability-injected equivalent exists or should be built for 'find the classloader's idea of where my own source file lives, then walk up N parents.' This is asking the runtime for ambient information about its own deployment layout, not asking a granted handle to read named data -- there is no host to inject a handle FROM until the module already knows where it is. Recommendation: documented exception, PLUS a design fix (require an explicit :root, drop the classpath-walk fallback) that this ADR recommends regardless of the clojure.java.io migration, since the module's own docstring already documents that fallback as producing plausible-looking wrong answers off the expected west layout.\n  - The `.deleteOnExit` half of pattern 5. There is no capability-injected equivalent for 'ask the JVM to clean this file up whenever the process happens to exit' -- that is JVM-process-lifecycle ambient authority, not filesystem authority, and it is also unavailable on Node/cljs entirely (no ADR needed to know this: it is simply not portable). Recommendation: the host that grants a tmp-fs handle also owns cleanup, e.g. via an explicit `with-open`-style scope, or a host-level supervisor that periodically clears its own tmp directory. Do not attempt to preserve 'silently self-cleans on exit' as a semantic; make cleanup an explicit, visible responsibility of whoever constructed the tmp-fs handle.\n  - The POSIX-permission-tightening half of pattern 8 (`.setReadable`/`.setWritable`). kotoba-lang/fs's IFilesystem has no chmod-equivalent operation at all, on either the protocol or fs-host's implementation, and the file's own comment already notes this cannot be done portably even in plain java.io ('the JVM cannot set POSIX permissions portably through spit'). Recommendation: do NOT silently drop this guarantee during migration -- either (i) add an explicit, host-only `IFilesystem` extension (e.g. a `chmod!` multimethod dispatched only from fs-host, refused with a typed error on any non-OS-backed impl like mem-filesystem, so a WASM guest cannot even attempt to ask for it) scoped narrowly to this one real need, or (ii) leave permission-tightening as a documented, separate, non-fs-mediated call the host itself makes immediately after `fs/write` returns, using the same java.nio/node:fs primitives fs-host already privately depends on -- i.e. push the permission call into the HOST'S OWN code (which already has full OS access, unlike the guest), not into the guest-visible capability surface. Either way, a migration of this site that drops the permission tightening silently is a regression, not a refactor, and this ADR recommends it be treated as a release-blocking test (assert the file mode after migration) rather than caught by review alone.\n  - Recursive directory walk and atomic rename (folder_sync.clj, encountered just past the 8-sampled-sites boundary but directly relevant): not migratable today because the primitives (b) lists as gaps do not exist yet. Recommendation: do not migrate folder_sync.clj in the same wave as the 8 sampled patterns; treat 'add recursive walk + atomic rename to fs-host' as its own follow-up proposal once the read-bytes/classpath-resource primitives above have already shipped and been used successfully, so the design of walk/rename benefits from that experience rather than being designed in the abstract.")
  (:adr/consequences
  "Approved by owner 2026-09-07: design accepted; implementation proceeds in the 7-step order below (step-1, landing fs/read-bytes on fs-host's host-filesystem, is underway as this approval's first hand; step-4 is the first clojure.java.io rewire wave). That ADR does not migrate any of the 3,320 clojure.java.io call sites; it is a design proposal for owner review, matching the task's explicit scope. If accepted, the recommended next steps in order are: (1) land `fs/read-bytes` on fs-host's host-filesystem (JVM + Node), the single smallest, lowest-risk addition that unblocks the most sampled patterns; (2) land a stream-chunk IReader/IWriter adapter in kotoba-lang/io for process/socket streams, coordinating with kotoba-lang/process's still-unbuilt spawn transport (named in adr-2809061500) so the two are not designed twice; (3) land a classpath-resource-backed IFilesystem (JVM-only, with a build-time-baked mem-filesystem as the cljs/WASM equivalent); (4) run the first migration wave (patterns 1, 3, 4 and their general class: scripts/, test/, docs generators) with before/after parity tests; (5) run the middle wave (patterns 5, 7) once (1)-(3) are proven; (6) treat pattern 6 (subprocess piping) and pattern 8 (signing-key file) as their own reviewed, tested changes, last, each with an explicit regression test for the silent-failure modes named in (d) (key regeneration, permission loosening); (7) treat folder_sync.clj (recursive walk + atomic rename) as a separate follow-up ADR once (1)-(3) have shipped, not as part of this wave. Accepting this ADR does NOT commit to a timeline for the other 3,312 sites; per adr-2809061500's own wave discipline, each subsequent wave is verified before the next, and this ADR's job is only to establish that the capability-injected shape is reachable for real call sites -- including the ones that need new primitives -- and to name, rather than paper over, the sites where it genuinely is not (pattern 2, and the two ambient-authority holes in pattern 5/8) so those are handled as explicit, reviewed exceptions rather than silently dropped guarantees.")))
